  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 239 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nA perturbation moving through the quasi-zonal flow along with \nincreased isentropic ascent along the nose of the low-level jet \ndeveloped storms as anticipated last evening and into the overnight \nhours. Veering of the LLJ has shifted the majority of activity \ninto Missouri with just some scattered showers and storms \nlingering over the forecast area. Forecast soundings show lift \nin the low cloud deck, favoring some drizzle and reduced \nvisibility across north central Kansas. Fog is also possible, \nalthough confidence in dense, widespread fog is low given the \ncloud cover. While some patchy dense fog is possible, think \ncloud cover will limit dense fog and preclude the need for a \nDense Fog Advisory.\n\nSkies gradually clear through the day with mostly sunny to partly \ncloudy skies expected by this afternoon. Solar insolation will help \ntemperatures rise into the 80s. A similar set-up to last evening/night\nwill evolve tonight as the LLJ again strengthens with more mid-level\nenergy passing overhead. CAMs differ in placement and coverage of\nstorms, but think something closer to the 00z HRRR makes sense conceptually,\nwith more widely scattered storms mainly along and south of Interstate\n70. A few storms may form during the late afternoon or evening with\ninverted-V soundings favorable for some gusty winds. The main concern\nfor any strong to severe storms comes after 9 PM through the early\nmorning hours Friday. The strongest storms could produce hail of\n1-1.25\", wind gusts around 60 MPH, and locally heavy rainfall. \n\nRain comes to an end by Friday afternoon as a mid-level ridge across \nthe Four Corners inches towards the Plains. 850mb temperatures warm \nto 22-24 degrees C, translating to surface temperatures in the upper \n80s to low 90s Friday afternoon. Highs could be a few degrees warmer \nif precipitation and clouds clear earlier than forecast. A surface \ntrough diving south across Iowa and Nebraska looks to be the impetus \nfor convective development Friday afternoon and evening with some \npotential for this activity to move into the area Friday evening and \nnight. Signals are mixed among guidance, so have maintained 10-30% \nPoPs.\n\nThe Central Plains remains on the eastern periphery of the mid-level \nridge this weekend into early next week. While this does lead to \ngradually hotter temperatures each day through Monday, it also \nallows the possibility for waves to ride the ridge and generate \ncloud cover and precipitation across the area. The GEFS, ENS, and \nGEPS ensembles show a 5-15 degrees spread between the 25th-75th \npercentiles for highs Saturday-Monday. The NBM depicts a tighter \nspread, but still shows the potential for highs in the upper 80s to \nlow 90s, if conditions are cloudier/wetter, or in the mid 90s to \naround 100 degrees, if dry weather and sunny skies dominate. The \nbest chances for precipitation may come during the overnight hours, \nleading to a higher chance for the hotter temperatures to \nmaterialize. The current forecast has heat index values of 95-105 \neach afternoon from Saturday through Monday, but there remains \nenough uncertainty to hold off on any headlines at this range. \n\nEnsembles favor the ridge flattening again by the middle of next \nweek with a surface front and embedded shortwaves leading to \nincreasing precipitation chances.",
